{"id": "sdqa0060", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 3.43, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2944, "eot_read_ms": 28, "reference_answer": "a class of organophosphorus compounds with the general structure O=P(OR)3", "query": "What is an organophosphate?", "transcript": "What is an organophosphate?", "mode": "local", "answer": "An organophosphate is a type of chemical compound that contains phosphorus, oxygen, and at least one organic group. These compounds are widely used as pesticides and insecticides in agriculture because they disrupt the nervous systems of insects. However, some organophosphates can also be toxic to humans and other animals if not handled properly. They work by inhibiting an enzyme called acetylcholinesterase, which plays a key role in nerve signal transmission.", "answer_ms": 2841}
