{"id": "sdqa0117", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "balanced", "audio_s": 6.54, "n_chunks": 7, "n_concurrent": 7, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.3036, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": "November 18, 1883", "query": "When were the North American time zones established?", "transcript": "When were the North American time zones established?", "mode": "local", "answer": "The North American time zones were established in the late 19th century, primarily due to the expansion of railroads and the need for a standardized system to coordinate train schedules. Before this, each city or region often kept its own local time based on the sun's position. The first major step toward standardization occurred in 1883 when the United States and Canada adopted a system of four time zones—Eastern, Central, Mountain, and Pacific—coordinated by railroad companies. This system was formalized over the following decades, with Canada officially adopting time zones in 1893 and further adjustments made as technology and transportation evolved.", "answer_ms": 4181}
